WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.792
Authentication changes what DAST can see.

2
00:00:02.792 --> 00:00:13.029
Many meaningful security issues appear only after login, inside workflows that use roles, tenants, preferences, account settings, or sensitive records.

3
00:00:13.029 --> 00:00:27.531
Authenticated DAST can evaluate session cookies, bearer tokens, CSRF protections at a high level, session expiration, access-controlled routes, and logged-in application behavior that public scanning cannot reach.

4
00:00:27.531 --> 00:00:31.176
Scanner authentication must be maintained carefully.

5
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:42.267
Login flows may include redirects, anti-automation controls, multifactor authentication considerations, session renewal, token expiration, and logout behavior.

6
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:50.255
A scanner that silently loses authentication may continue testing as an anonymous user and produce misleading coverage.

7
00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:57.700
Test plans should define how credentials or tokens are supplied, protected, rotated, and removed after testing.

8
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:04.447
Role-based test accounts are important because one account rarely represents the whole application.

9
00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:12.202
A standard user, manager, support user, tenant administrator, and read-only user may each reach different surfaces.

10
00:01:12.202 --> 00:01:24.533
Testing tenant boundaries and object-level access control requires accounts and data that can demonstrate whether a user is limited to authorized records, objects, files, or resources.

11
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:26.162
Account safety matters.

12
00:01:26.162 --> 00:01:34.925
DAST can trigger account lockouts, notifications, audit events, rate limits, or workflow side effects if accounts are not prepared.

13
00:01:34.925 --> 00:01:42.758
Test credentials and tokens are sensitive because they may provide access to application functionality and test data.

14
00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:50.436
They should not be stored in plaintext logs, committed to repositories, or reused outside the approved test context.
